Render a frame as soon as the picture has changed, instead of on the next beat of the frame
rate, and never more frames in one second than the frame rate. Off unless asked for.

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86. The lines of context are from that project.

It is kept small and in a shape that can be proposed to that project
(docs/adr/0009-host-compositor.md): the element `waylanddisplaysrc` gets a property,
`render-on-commit`, that is off by default, and with it off nothing changes. The rules are in
one new file with its tests, wayland-display-core/src/comp/pacing.rs, which also says why.

What the mode does:

- A frame the element has asked for is rendered as soon as a client has committed or the
  pointer has moved. If nothing changes, it goes out after two frame intervals as it is, so
  that the stream does not stop.
- In no one second do more frames start rendering than the frame rate says. A frame this rule
  would hold back for more than a millisecond gives way to the next picture.
- Clients are told to draw their next frame (wl_surface.frame) when they have committed, but
  not more often than once a frame interval. A client that draws whenever it is told to, as
  most applications do, then commits once per interval, and its picture goes out when it
  arrives instead of waiting for the next beat.
- When the stream ends, the compositor says in its log how many frames started rendering and
  how many of them at most in any one second.

Tests (`cargo test -p wayland-display-core`): twelve of the rules, played against model clients
(one that draws when told, ones that keep a rhythm of their own), and four with a real Wayland
client.

Earlier versions, in the history of this file. The first rendered on a commit and kept the old
timer as well: 108 to 118 frames a second where 85 were asked for. The second kept to the frame
rate but told clients to draw with every frame. A client that draws when told then drew as fast
as it could: the frames of a second were gone in its first part and the picture stood still for
the rest (docs/measurements/2026-11-16-step3-pacing.md). Our test application keeps a rhythm of
its own and did not show it.

Known limits:

- The one-second rule costs a client that keeps exactly the frame rate up to its own
  unsteadiness: a picture that comes early for its place in the second waits until the second
  is over.
- Nothing is exposed through the C bindings yet.

Apply and build, next to the unpatched checkout:

    git worktree add ../repo-commit3 1301f86
    cd ../repo-commit3 && git apply <this file>
    cargo build --release -p gst-plugin-wayland-display -p wayland-display-core --features cuda

